<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erasmus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1466–1536</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Desiderius Erasmus, Erasmus of Rotterdam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> Erasmus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> humanist scholar, the most famous of the Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. His Greek edition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> of 1516, his satire The Praise of Folly and his many letters and editions of the Church Fathers made him a leading voice for reform within the Catholic Church. He criticised abuses but refused to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>, and his tolerant middle way influenced rulers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erasmus.jpg" alt="Erasmus" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Holbein the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Holbein-erasmus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
